    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nAfter #40270, events are no longer emitted from the automatic background detection. This applies not just during startup, but also if the user manually changes the background of their terminal.\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nSet the background as normal, assuming that a normal terminal will respond within 100 ms. Change test to match expected behavior:\r\n- BG set during startup won't trigger user autocmds since it runs before any user config\r\n- If the terminal takes longer than 100 ms to respond to initial OSC 11, it does trigger the OptionSet, but it is triggered through the normal path to ensure values like v:option_new are set #38551\r\n- BG change after startup still triggers autocmds #41146\r\n\r\nI ran the [new test 100 times](https://github.com/kylesower/neovim/actions/runs/31270083775) to make sure it isn't flaky. The fundamental issue with the old test is that it was dependent on the timing of the OSC 11 response, but I think the expected behavior of \"does not trigger OptionSet during startup\" doesn't matter. Looking at it more closely, the old test didn't even test that because `-c` commands run after `nlua_init_defaults()`.\r\n\r\nrelated #39830\r\nclose #41146",
